WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.280
With over two dozen different Marvel movies
and more than a few Marvel television shows,

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.400
we've seen some incredible comic-book-based
performances from some of Hollywood's very

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.100
best actors.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.020
Not all of those actors are still with us,
but thankfully, they've left behind a long

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.340
and impressive body of work even beyond their
Marvel roles.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.920
Here are Marvel actors you may not know passed
away.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:23.600
Powers Boothe

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.330
If you don't recognize Powers Boothe from
his brief appearance in The Avengers or his

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:33.200
run on Marvel's Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D, you
probably know him as cult leader Jim Jones

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.320
in Guyana Tragedy, a role that won him an
Emmy.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.900
You may also know him as the conniving brothel
owner Cy Tolliver on HBO's Deadwood or as

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.300
24's Vice President Noah Daniels.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.370
Marvel fans, however, will always see Boothe
as Gideon Malick, the shadowy world leader

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.580
that was first seen in The Avengers, where
he encouraged Nick Fury to nuke New York City

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.860
in order to stop Loki's alien army.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.730
Malick later returned for Marvel's Agents
of S.H.I.E.L.D. as season three's big bad

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.000
and the head of HYDRA.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.340
"You have less than 15 minutes to breach their
barricades and regain control of that chamber."

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.539
"Sir, I really-"
"That's an order, soldier."

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.860
Malick isn't the only comic book character
that Boothe played, either.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.060
He voiced Gorilla Grodd and Red Tornado in
Justice League Unlimited, played Lex Luthor

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:23.000
in the animated feature Superman: Brainiac
Attacks, and appeared in both Sin City movies

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.860
as Senator Roark.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.380
Sadly, Boothe passed away in May 2017.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.800
Onscreen villainy will never be the same.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.020
Roy Scheider

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.960
When a group of thugs show up at the Castle
family reunion looking to exact revenge in

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.259
2004's The Punisher, Roy Schieder's Frank
Castle Sr. is there to stop them in their

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:43.760
tracks.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.560
Not that you'd expect anything less from the
man who faced down Jaws not once, but twice,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.060
and lived to tell the story.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.219
As Police Chief Martin Brody, Scheider is
one of the three men who take to the seas

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.680
in order to stop the tourist-eating great
white.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.480
Reportedly, Scheider even improvised Brody's
most famous line:

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.720
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.900
He left behind an impressive resume beyond
Jaws though.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.700
He received an Academy Award nomination for
playing grizzled Detective "Cloudy" Russo

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.500
in The French Connection, and another for
his role in All That Jazz.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.140
Scheider passed away in 2008.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.300
Garry Shandling

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.959
In Iron Man 2, Garry Shandling's Senator Stern
tries to discredit Iron Man and transfer Tony

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:41.180
Stark's tech for the United States government
but the extent of his villainy becomes even

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.020
more established in Captain America: The Winter
Soldier:

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.460
"Hail HYDRA."

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.640
It's one of the most memorable parts of Marvel
movie history, but it's barely a footnote

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.860
in Shandling's long and accomplished career.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.760
Shandling started his career as a writer for
'70s sitcoms, but decided to pursue stand-up

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.260
comedy.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.220
Appearances on The Tonight Show landed Shandling
a gig as one of Johnny Carson's go-to guest

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.140
hosts, and eventually led to the subversive,
fourth-wall breaking sitcom It's Garry Shandling's

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:07.640
Show.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.920
Later, Shandling funneled his Tonight Show
experience into The Larry Sanders Show, an

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.600
HBO series about a late night talk show.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:20.760
Over the course of its six-season run, The
Larry Sanders Show amassed a whopping 56 Emmy

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.360
nominations, helped make Jeffrey Tambor, Rip
Torn, and Janeane Garofalo into household

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.050
names, and cemented its place as one of the
greatest television shows ever made.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.700
Unfortunately, Shandling passed away in 2016.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:34.580
Bill Paxton

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.310
Fan favorite character actor Bill Paxton added
a helpful dose of starpower to Marvel's Agents

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.750
of S.H.I.E.L.D when he brought the cyborg
assassin John Garrett to life onscreen for

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.880
the very first time in 2014.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.860
"Why do I hear gunfire?"

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.640
"You hear the dying breath of an old world,
general.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.740
But a new world is coming.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.820
I've tasted it on my tongue."

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.960
As Garrett, Paxton lured straight-laced S.H.I.E.L.D
agent Grant Ward to the dark side, uncovered

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.630
the secret behind Phil Coulson's mysterious
resurrection, and ultimately ended up on the

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.880
wrong side of Coulson's disintegration gun.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:13.220
Unfortunately, Paxton, who made his name as
a scene-stealing presence in classic movies

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.880
like Aliens, Terminator, Edge of Tomorrow,
True Lies, and many, many others, passed away

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.320
just a few years later due to complications
resulting from heart surgery.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:24.780
Neil Fingleton

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.620
A British athlete-turned-actor who stood at
a record-setting seven feet, seven inches

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.660
tall, Neil Fingleton was officially the tallest
man in Britain.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.430
He played college basketball at the University
of North Carolina and College of the Holy

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:44.259
Cross, represented Britain in the World University
Games, and later played professionally in

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.780
Boston, Italy, Spain, and Greece.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.560
However, Fingleton's sports career came to
an abrupt end in 2007, when an injury finished

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.520
his time on the court.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.980
He turned to acting, where his massive size
landed him all kinds of unique roles.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:03.240
In Doctor Who, Fingleton appeared opposite
Peter Capaldi as the would-be conqueror Fisher

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:03.740
King.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.699
He also played Game of Thrones' resident giant,
Mag the Mighty.

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:11.650
On the Marvel side, Fingleton actually played
two roles.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:16.370
Fingleton helped bring Ultron to life, working
as both a stunt performer and motion capture

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.140
artist on Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.140
He also played a Russian bodyguard in X-Men:
First Class.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.940
He died of heart failure in February of 2017.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.060
Cliff Robertson

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.910
Compared to the rest of the cast in Spider-Man,
Cliff Robertson's Uncle Ben might not have

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.250
the most screen time, but it's his teachings
that ultimately inspire Peter Parker to become

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:37.760
Spider-Man.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.300
"Remember, with great power comes great responsibility."

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.620
Much like Ben, Robertson was a real-life hero.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.680
While he stole hearts as a young John F. Kennedy
in P.T. 109 and won an Oscar for the lead

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.620
role in Charly, he's probably best known for
taking a stand against Hollywood corruption.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:02.060
In 1977, Robertson reported that Columbia
Pictures head David Begelman had forged his

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:05.760
name on a $10,000 check as part of a massive
embezzling scheme.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:11.500
In the aftermath, Begelman lost his job, and
Hollywood bigwigs refused to give Robertson

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.260
more parts.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.220
Robertson never fully embraced the show business
machine, and he wasn't sorry for his actions

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:19.490
shining a light on Hollywood corruption.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.020
Robertson passed away in 2011.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.080
Harry Dean Stanton

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.370
If you don't know Harry Dean Stanton's name,
you certainly know his face.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:32.440
In the '60s and '70s, he slowly emerged as
one of Hollywood's most reliable character

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.930
actors, thanks to memorable bit parts in films
and television shows like Cool Hand Luke,

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.880
Gunsmoke, and The Godfather Part II.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.160
Stanton's popularity eventually lead to bigger
supporting roles, including parts in Alien,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.280
Escape from New York, and The Green Mile.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.610
In 1984, he took the lead in the critically
acclaimed Paris, Texas, and the same year,

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.280
he co-starred as Bud in the cult-classic sci-fi
romp Repo Man.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:00.560
He's also notable for playing a recurring
part in Twin Peaks: The Return, his second-to-last

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.280
project before his September 2017 passing.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:08.340
In between his packed schedule, Stanton found
time to sneak into the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.020
with a scene-stealing performance as a wise
janitor in The Avengers.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.180
"From outer space.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:13.820
An alien?"

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:15.040
"Nah."

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:18.890
"Well then son, you've got a condition."

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.360
Evgeniy "Eugene" Lazarev

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.800
In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Anton Vanko
was a longtime Stark Industries employee who

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.599
helped develop the Arc Reactor before being
found guilty of espionage and shipped back

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:32.160
to Russia.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:38.259
After a lengthy stint in Siberia, Vanko returned
to civilization to raise his son, turning

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:43.440
Ivan Vanko against the Starks and creating
Iron Man 2's main villain, Whiplash.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.500
Thankfully, the actor who played Vanko, Evgeniy
"Eugene" Lazarev, wasn't quite so twisted

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.380
in real life.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:53.880
A Russian native, Lazarev was a well-known
and accomplished actor in his motherland,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.820
where he was honored with the People's Artist
of Russia title, the most prestigious government

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.780
award a Russian performer can attain.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.139
On his move to the United States, Lazarev
kept right on acting, appearing in films and

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:12.000
TV shows like The Pink Panther 2, 24, Alias,
The West Wing, Lord of War and many others.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:17.860
He didn't keep all of that experience to himself,
either, between roles, Lazarev taught acting

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.500
at the University of Southern California.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:22.220
Sadly, he died of a heart attack in 2016.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:24.380
Michael Clarke Duncan

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:30.260
When you're casting someone like New York
crime boss Wilson Fisk, better known to superhero

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.460
fans as Kingpin, you need someone who's got
a huge presence.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.539
Michael Clarke Duncan didn't just look the
part with his six foot, 300 pound frame, he

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.040
also had charisma to spare.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:44.600
The actor's stature helped him land the gig
as the big bad in 2003's Daredevil.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:46.120
"Have you seen the papers today?

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:48.540
Somebody's been talking."

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.620
"Somebody always does."

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:57.440
Duncan, a superhero fan since childhood, was
very enthusiastic about playing Fisk.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:02.880
He re-read Frank Miller's comic book run from
front to back before filming Daredevil, and

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.130
returned to voice the character in Spider-Man:
The New Animated Series.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.520
Still, the Kingpin was just one of many memorable
parts Duncan played over the years.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:16.200
Duncan appeared in films like Armageddon,
The Whole Nine Yards, Sin City, and others,

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:21.191
usually playing criminals or other heavies,
but his star-making turn came in 1999 when

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.940
he joined the cast of Frank Darabont's The
Green Mile.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:29.540
Duncan landed the role as the imposing but
gentle inmate John Coffey, and was rewarded

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.760
for his efforts with a handful of award nominations,
including one for Best Supporting Actor.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:37.360
Duncan passed away from a heart attack in
September 2012.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:39.280
Bill Bixby

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:45.760
Visit any reasonably sized comic book convention,
and you'll find that Lou Ferrigno, the six-foot-five

00:09:45.779 --> 00:09:51.380
bodybuilder who played Marvel's jade giant
on the 1977 Incredible Hulk television show,

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:55.180
is alive, well, and still signing autographs
for adoring fans.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:00.360
Unfortunately, his onscreen alter ego, Bill
Bixby, is no longer with us.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:05.940
Bixby will always be best known for his portrayal
of David "Bruce" Banner, the heroic scientist

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.690
who traveled the country in search of a cure
for his condition.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:09.680
"Mr. Mcgee.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:10.540
Don't make me angry.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:12.900
You wouldn't like me when I'm angry."

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.070
Even without the Marvel bona fides, he'd be
well-remembered.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:21.440
In 1971, he was nominated for a Best Actor
Emmy for his ongoing role in The Courtship

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.820
of Eddie's Father, and followed that up in
1976 with two more Emmy nods.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:30.960
He was an accomplished director, helming 30
episodes of Blossom ,as well as other television

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:31.460
shows.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.560
Bixby's showmanship extended offscreen, too.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.660
He performed all of his own tricks while starring
in NBC's The Magician.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:43.720
Sadly, Bixby passed away in 1993 after a two-year
battle with cancer.

